    "created_at": "2022-07-05T16:31:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-07-05T16:32:07Z",
    "body": "Excluding whitespace from default slot assignment would impact some of our elements. I doubt we’re alone there and it seems pretty likely it would mostly impact folks who don’t realize they’ve been relying on the existing behavior. (It’s also a little unclear how it would work since whether to assign a whitespace-only text node would end up dependent on siblings and their current `data` content.)\r\n\r\nI’m pretty sure manual slotting would permit something akin to the behavior you’re looking for, but until that’s more broadly supported, would named slots be an option?\r\n\r\nAnother possibility is transforming/preprocessing the HTML: some folks remove “indentation whitespace”, possibly conditionally based on context, as part of a build pipeline. This works out okay most of the time, but I would only recommend it if you’re sure the transformation will occur the same in development as in production since whitespace has lots of potential effects in HTML & CSS — speaking of which:\r\n\r\n> everybody is used to format HTML code without worrying of meaningless strings as spaces, tabs and new lines.\r\n\r\nI think you might’ve gotten lucky! HTML’s ability to surprise folks with whitespace effects is one of its oldest “features”. The billions of extra spaces between “ <a href=\"#\">links</a> ” and punctuation intended to follow or enclose em on the web can be viewed as some sort of testament to this tradition :)",
    "author_association": "NONE",
